2. Classical Tracking Algorithms

If you are tracking by color density, movement profiles, or raw features rather than structural appearance bounding boxes, OpenCV includes dedicated mathematical implementations:
• CamShift & MeanShift (cv2.CamShift, cv2.meanShift): Best for color-histogram based tracking (like tracking an orange ball or a specific colored shirt).
• Optical Flow (cv2.calcOpticalFlowPyrLK): Evaluates vector displacement of specific feature points (Sparse/KLT) or all pixels (Dense) across frames.



camshift is probably the better option fo this use



i-object CamShift tracking (red ball, yellow hat, blue hat)
Approach
Run one independent CamShift tracker per color, inside color_rec_video.py (Picamera2 loop).

Use the existing HSV inRange masks (red = two ranges OR'd for hue wrap) as the CamShift "back-projection" image instead of a hand-picked ROI histogram. This avoids the red hue-wrap problem and needs no manual ROI selection.
Per frame: blur/open the mask, then for each tracker:
If tracking: cv2.CamShift(mask, window, term_crit).
If the pixel sum inside the returned window is below a threshold (lost) or no window yet: re-acquire from the largest contour (area > 300, as already used) and set a new window.
Draw a rotated box + label per object in its color; skip drawing when lost.
Keep the existing LED logic (largest visible color drives color_change).
Todos
Refactor masks into a dict {name: (mask, box_color)} for Red/Yellow/Blue.
Add a Tracker class (window, lost flag, update(mask) -> box or None).
Replace detect_color drawing with tracker updates and cv2.boxPoints drawing (use np.intp, not deprecated np.int0).
Preserve the LED selection using each tracker's window area.
Test on the Pi with all three objects, occlusion, and re-entry.
Live top-down map (added)
Yes, easy with OpenCV: draw on a separate blank numpy image and cv2.imshow("Map", map_img) each frame.

Camera sits at bottom-center of the map, looking up; a FOV wedge is drawn (Pi camera ~62 deg horizontal).
Bearing from the tracker's box center: angle = (cx - W/2)/W * HFOV.
Distance from apparent size: d = k / sqrt(area) (or known real width: d = f*realW/pixelW); calibrate k once by holding each object at a measured distance. Rough estimate only, not exact.
Map position: mx = cx0 + d*sin(angle)*scale, my = cy0 - d*cos(angle)*scale.
Colored dot + label per object (red/yellow/blue); hide when lost.
Fading trail: keep a short deque of past points per object, or multiply the map by ~0.95 each frame (cv2.addWeighted) before drawing the new dots, so old positions fade.
Range rings every 0.5 m and a grid for readability.
Todo: map-view (depends on draw-led).
Notes
Capture uses cv2.VideoCapture(0) (not picamera2); new file multi_track_map.py, keeps color_rec_video.py untouched. LED calls (color_change) are optional and wrapped in try/except for non-Pi use.
Lost grace period: ~5 frames before an object is declared lost.
CamShift can't separate two same-colored objects; fine here since all three have different hues.
Yellow range (H 18-40) may overlap skin/wood; tune S/V or add a minimum-area filter.
Picamera2 "RGB888" is actually BGR in memory, so the existing BGR2HSV is correct.
Optional: circularity check for the ball to reject red clutter.




Added multi_track_map.py. It uses cv2.VideoCapture(0) and one CamShift tracker per color (red ball, yellow hat, blue hat). It re-acquires a lost object from the largest blob after a 5-frame grace period. A second "Map" window shows a top-down view with a field-of-view wedge, range rings, labeled dots and a fading trail. I tested the tracking and map math on a synthetic image only, with no real camera. Calibrate DIST_K (distance estimate), set HFOV_DEG for your camera, and tune the HSV ranges for your lighting. Run with python multi_track_map.py and press q to quit.


